course: dictionary
lesson: 5703
title: Words that paint a picture
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words paint a picture with their movement. Lollop is an informal verb, the kind of word you hear in chatty talk and funny stories. To lollop means to move along in a clumsy, bouncing, loose way, like a big puppy or a rabbit.
explain: Look at the context. «The Camel's back puffed up into a great big lolloping humph.» The word lolloping tells you the humph is big, bouncing and floppy, not neat or tidy. Picture a big friendly puppy running across the grass with its ears flapping: that is the feeling of lollop.
explain: Lollop is a verb, so its forms change: lollops, lolloped, lolloping. Ask yourself: how would a dog move if it lollops across a field? It moves loosely and happily, not in a straight, careful line.
fact: Lollop is a verb, and it is informal, so you hear it in everyday talk and stories more than in formal writing.
fact: «Lollop» means to move along in a clumsy, bouncing, loose way.
fact: Dogs, hares and other big, friendly creatures can all lollop.
fact: In Kipling's Just So Stories, the Camel's back puffs up into a great lolloping «humph».
exercise: «The puppy came lollopping across the grass, ears flapping.» What does lollopping mean? | *Moving along in a clumsy, bouncing, loose way | Sleeping under a tree | Swimming fast in a straight line | Standing very still and quiet
exercise: Which is the best example of lollop? | *A big floppy puppy bouncing across the park | A tap dripping in the sink | A clock ticking on the wall | A pencil lying still on a desk
exercise: Which word is the past tense of lollop? | lollops | lollop | *lolloped | lolloping
test: «The Camel's back puffed up into a great big lolloping humph.» Why might the author choose lolloping? | *To paint a picture of a big, floppy, bouncing puff | To show the humph is very quiet | To say the Camel is very cold | To say the Camel is asleep
test: Mia says lollop is a fancy word for skipping. What is the better answer? | Lollop means to swim quickly | Lollop means exactly the same as skip, so there is no difference | Lollop means to shout loudly | *Lollop means bouncing loosely and clumsily, not neat skipping
exam: Which word is an informal verb meaning to move along in a clumsy, bouncing, loose way? | glum | barely | *lollop | ancient
